        "excerpt": "T-Wave Generation and Seismic Signal Propagation in Oceanic Environments - Recent articles and discoveries | Springer Nature Link\nSkip to main content\nLog in\nMenu\nFind a journal\nPublish with us\nTrack your research\nSearch\nSaved research\nCart\nHome\nSubjects\nT\nSubject\nT-Wave Generation and Seismic Signal Propagation in Oceanic Environments\nUncover the latest and most impactful research in T-Wave Generation and Seismic Signal Propagation in Oceanic Environments. Explore pioneering discoveries, insightful ideas and new methods from leading researchers in the field.\nHighlights from latest Springer Nature content\nAI generated\nT-waves from magnitude-3.5 and magnitude-3.7 earthquakes off northeastern Brazil showed efficient seismic-energy coupling into the SOFAR channel from the continental slope, despite no seismic T-waves being recorded at neighbouring coastal land stations.\n(Melo & Tavares, 2026)\nT-wave energy and duration were particularly sensitive to seafloor slope, seabed properties and earthquake depth and position relative to the slope in spectral-element simulations of a typical downslope generation scenario.\n(Bottero et al., 2020)\nHydroacoustic phases recorded at seismic arrays and infrasound stations exhibited expected azimuth estimates, and these networks may detect hydroacoustic signals in regions not covered by the International Monitoring System hydroacoustic network.\n(Bittner et al., 2024)\nAcoustic–gravity wave penetration into an elastic sea-bottom modifies propagation speed and hydrophone arrival times, affecting estimates of where objects impacted the sea surface.\n(Kadri, 2019)\nHydroacoustic recordings from a seafloor observatory and Wake Island yielded location and origin-time estimates for signals deemed associated with an F-35A crash off Japan, with the location matching the aircraft’s last known position.\n(Metz et al., 2022)\nThis content was generated using AI. Check important information.\nRate these highlights:\nTell us more (opens survey in a new tab)\nLatest research\nArticle\nT-wave generation and propagation in the South China Sea from multi-source seismic data\nZhuo Xiao\nYue Wang\nMin Xu\nin\nScience China Earth Sciences\nT-waves, or tertiary waves, are hydroacoustic signals that propagate through the ocean after seismic energy couples from the solid Earth into the water column. The Sound Fixing and Ranging (SOFAR) channel traps hydroacoustic energy, enabling T waves...\n24 June 2026\nArticle\nEfficient T-wave generation by small-magnitude earthquakes along the NE Brazil continental margin\nGuilherme W. S. de Melo\nAline C. Tavares\nin\nJournal of Seismology\nIn 2021 and 2022, two offshore small-magnitude earthquakes (3.5 and 3.7) occurred in the northeastern Brazilian continental margin. Both occurrences produced hydroacoustic T-waves, which were recorded by H10N and H10S hydrophone arrays located near...\n29 April 2026\nOpen access\nArticle\nHydroacoustic Signals Detected at Coastal International Monitoring System Stations\nPaulina Bittner\nSherif M. Ali\nIvana Jukic\nin\nPure and Applied Geophysics\nEleven stations, which belong to the International Monitoring System (IMS) hydroacoustic network, are specifically designed for detecting underwater nuclear test explosions. Nearly half of the hydroacoustic network consists of T-phase stations near...\n02 November 2024\nArticle\nRemote Hydroacoustic Detection of an Airplane Crash\nDirk Metz\nKoichiro Obana\nYoshio Fukao\nin\nPure and Applied Geophysics\nOn 9 April 2019, an F-35A fighter jet of the Japan Air Self-Defense Force was lost offshore northern Honshu, Japan. Underwater sound phases deemed to be associated with the crash of the aircraft were recorded by a nearby seafloor observatory and the...\n13 September 2022\nArticle\nMulti-phase seismic source imprint of tropical cyclones\nLise Retailleau\nLucia Gualtieri\nin\nNature Communications\nThe coupling between the ocean activity driven by winds and the solid Earth generates seismic signals recorded by seismometers worldwide. The 2–10 s period band, known as secondary microseism, represents the largest background seismic wavefield....\n06 April 2021\nOpen access\nArticle\nOn the Influence of Slopes, Source, Seabed and Water Column Properties on T Waves: Generation at Shore\nAlexis Bottero\nPaul Cristini\nDimitri Komatitsch\nin\nPure and Applied Geophysics\nThe term T waves is generally associated with acoustic waves generated by seismic events that subsequently travel horizontally in the ocean at the speed of sound. In this paper, we use a time-domain spectral-element method to perform a parametric...\n04 November 2020\nArticle\nCalculation of Hydroacoustic Propagation and Conversion to Seismic Phases at T-Stations\nJeffry L. Stevens\nJeffrey Hanson\nSteven M. Day\nin\nPure and Applied Geophysics\nThe International Monitoring System (IMS) hydroacoustic network consists of six hydrophone stations and 5 T-stations. The IMS T-stations are high-frequency seismic stations (sample rates of 100 Hz) situated on islands or coastal stations and...\n13 August 2020\nOpen access\nArticle\nCTBTO’s Data and Analysis Pertaining to the Search for the Missing Argentine Submarine ARA San Juan\nPeter L. Nielsen\nMario Zampolli\nNurcan Meral Özel\nin\nPure and Applied Geophysics\nThe Argentine submarine ARA (Armada de la República Argentina) San Juan went missing on November 15th, 2017, with its last confirmed contact location around 600 kilometres East of Comodoro Rivadavia, Chubut, Argentina. In the days following the...\n03 March 2020\nOpen access\nArticle\nEffect of sea-bottom elasticity on the propagation of acoustic–gravity waves from impacting objects\nUsama Kadri\nin\nScientific Reports\nRecent analysis of data, recorded on March 8th 2014 at the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty Organisation’s hydroacoustic stations off Cape Leeuwin Western Australia, and at Diego Garcia, has led to the development of an inverse model for...\n29 January 2019\nOpen access\nArticle\nRewinding the waves: tracking underwater signals to their source\nUsama Kadri\nDavide Crivelli\nAmanda Ryan\nin\nScientific Reports\nAnalysis of data, recorded on March 8th 2014 at the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty Organisation’s hydroacoustic stations off Cape Leeuwin Western Australia, and at Diego Garcia, reveal unique pressure signatures that could be associated with...\n24 October 2017\nOpen access\nConference paper\nPile-Driving Pressure and Particle Velocity at the Seabed: Quantifying Effects on Crustaceans and Groundfish\nJames H. Miller\nGopu R. Potty\nHui-Kwan Kim\nin\nThe Effects of Noise on Aquatic Life II\nWe modeled the effects of pile driving on crustaceans, groundfish, and other animals near the seafloor. Three different waves were investigated, including the compressional wave, shear wave, and interface wave. A finite element (FE) technique was...\n2016\nArticle\nIdentification of T-Waves in the Alboran Sea\nEnrique Carmona\nJavier Almendros\nJesús M. Ibáñez\nin\nPure and Applied Geophysics\nAnalyses of seismograms from ~1,100 north-Moroccan earthquakes recorded at stations of the Red Sísmica de Andalucía (Southern Spain) reveal the systematic presence of late phases embedded in the earthquake codas. These phases have distinctive...\n17 January 2015\nArticle\nDetection of small earthquakes along the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge from T-waves recorded by abyssal ocean-bottom observatories\nAki Ito\nHiroko Sugioka\nYoshio Fukao\nin\nMarine Geophysical Research\nA number of seismoacoustic T-wave events were observed between January 2003 and January 2004 by broadband ocean-bottom seismometers installed on the French Polynesia seafloor at depths of 4,000–5,000 m, well below the conjugate depth of the SOFAR...\n18 July 2012\nArticle\nHydroacoustic contributions to understanding the December 26th 2004 great Sumatra–Andaman Earthquake\nMaya Tolstoy\nDelWayne R. Bohnenstiehl\nin\nSurveys in Geophysics\nWithin the era of modern digital-recording, the December 26th 2004 Sumatra–Andaman Earthquake represents an event of unprecedented scale. Hydroacoustic observations have made significant contributions toward our understanding of this great rupture...\n01 August 2006\nArticle\nT-phase Observations in Northern California: Acoustic to Seismic Coupling at a Weakly Elastic Boundary\nC. deGroot-Hedlin\nJ. Orcutt\nin\npure and applied geophysics\n— Plans for a hydroacoustic network intended to monitor compliance with the CTBT call for the inclusion of five T-phase stations situated at optimal locations for the detection of seismic phases converted from ocean-borne Tphases. We examine...\n01 March 2001\nArticle\nAnalysis of Russian Hydroacoustic Data for CTBT Monitoring\nM. Eneva\nJ.L. Stevens\nV.V. Adushkin\nin\npure and applied geophysics\n— As part of a collaborative research program for the purpose of monitoring the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty (CTBT), we are in the process of examining and analyzing hydroacoustic data from underwater explosions conducted in the former...\n01 March 2001\nArticle\nOperational Processing of Hydroacoustics at the Prototype International Data Center\nJ. Hanson\nR. Le Bras\nJ. Guern\nin\npure and applied geophysics\n— The Prototype International Data Center (PIDC) has designed and implemented a system to process data from the International Monitoring System's hydroacoustic network. The automatic system detects and measures various signal characteristics that...\n01 March 2001\nArticle\nEmpirical and Numerical Modeling of T-phase Propagation from Ocean to Land\nJ.L. Stevens\nG.E. Baker\nS.M. Day\nin\npure and applied geophysics\n— T-phase propagation from ocean onto land is investigated by comparing data from hydrophones in the water column with data from the same events recorded on island and coastal seismometers. Several events located on Hawaii and the emerging seamount...\n01 March 2001\nArticle\nNormal Mode Composition of Earthquake T Phases\nG.L. D'Spain\nL.P. Berger\nG.E. Baker\nin\npure and applied geophysics\n— Understanding the nature of the coupling between the underwater acoustic field and the land seismic field is important for evaluating the performance of the T-phase stations in the International Monitoring System for the Comprehensive...\n01 March 2001\nArticle\nConverted T Phases Recorded on Hawaii from Polynesian Nuclear Tests: A Preliminary Report\nE.A. Okal\nin\npure and applied geophysics\n— We present a preliminary study of Twaves from Polynesian nuclear tests at Mururoa, recorded on digital stations of the Hawaii Volcano Observatory network, following their conversion to seismic waves at the southern shore of the Island of Hawaii,...\n01 March 2001\nView all research\nHow was your experience today?\nShare feedback (opens in new tab)\nSearch\nSearch by keyword or author\nSearch\nNavigation\nFind a journal\nPublish with us\nTrack your research\nFooter Navigation\nDiscover content\nJournals A-Z\nBooks A-Z\nSubjects A-Z\nPublish with us\nJournal finder\nPublish your research\nLanguage editing\nOpen access publishing\nProducts and services\nOur products\nLibrarians\nSocieties\nPartners and advertisers\nOur brands\nSpringer\nNature Portfolio\nBMC\nPalgrave Macmillan\nApress\nDiscover\nCorporate Navigation\nYour privacy choices/Manage cookies\nYour US state privacy rights\nAccessibility statement\nTerms and conditions\nPrivacy policy\nHelp and support\nLegal notice\nCancel contracts here\n132.196.37.211\nNot affiliated\n© 2026 Springer Nature",
